    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-2">Unexpected Application of ML Theory to Language Learning</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">March 12, 2026</p>
    <p>
      I can't think of a better example of a scientist more limited by their time. Today, people derisively refer to LLMs as "stochastic parrots" due to the randomness they use. But in 1948, Claude Shannon was deliberately trying to invent stochastic parrots. He obviously didn't have a computer, so he was stuck executing his algorithms by hand and got his random numbers a book containing tables of them. Despite these limitations, he came up with 6 "approximations" of English, each more realistic than the last.
    </p>
