    <p>
      You've probably noticed that computers have learned to talk recently. But plugged-in readers will know that it is only even more recently that we taught computers to spell. Until relatievly recently, LLMs capable of solving complex coding challenges would utterly choke on any question that depended on knowledge of how a word is spelled.
    </p>
    <p>
      This was surprising to a lot of people, because it seems in contradiction with the LLM's ability to respond with perfect spelling. But in-the-know readers will already understand the resolution to the paradox. The LLMs could never spell, because spelling-related tasks are handled by a "dumb" pre- and post-processing step in the pipeline: tokenization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Yes, when you look at a word, you can easily see a sequence of letters, but an LLM cannot, nor does it write words by writing sequences of letters. The best way to understand the LLM might be via an analogy. All letters are mostly-random shapes with no inherent meaning, but you've learned to recognize them. But imagine someone took a common pairing of letters, like "th", and then combined them into a new shape: Þ. So all instances of "th" were replaced with "Þ". If you learned to write like Þis, you'd probaby see noÞing wrong with it. It would seem as natural to you as any oÞer letter in english.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now imagine if they did this thousands of times. You would quickly have a symbol for every common pair of leters, such as "th", "qu", "oo", "ck", and so on. But there's a twist. When "the" became "Þ", that all occurances of "the" became "Þe". And that becomes a candidate for being merged as well - so "Þe" would become its own new symbol as well. Now imagine we continued this process, combining the most common pair of letters into a new letter, over and over, until we have 32,000 letters.
    </p>
    <p>
      So, in this hypothetical, you've been brought up to write like this and you know all of those 32,000 letters easily. The classic A-Z are still letters, but at this point most whole words have been collapsed into single symbols.
    </p>
    <p>
      And now you're working as an assistant, where people send you written questions and you type something in respose. But, they're writing questions in standard english and expect responses in standard english, while you're used to this language with 32,000 symbols. To stop this from being a problem for you, a dumb script translates all questinos into the symbol language for you, and then translates your responses back into standard english.
    </p>
    <p>
      99% of the time, this would be fine. If someone asked you "What is the capitol of France", that would be translated into your symbol language, and probably all of those words would have their own symbols. But you would know those symbols well, because you were raised with them. You could respond in terms of those same symbols, "The capitol of France is Paris," and it would be translatd back.
    </p>
    <p>
      But now imagine someone asked you "how many letters are in the word 'strawberry'?" "Strawberry" would be collapsed into a single symbol, so you would not have access to the obivous way of knowing (just counting the letters). You'd have to know, from having read people say it explicitly, that the word "strawberry" has 10 letters.
    </p>
    <p>
      And that would basically be a separaet piece of information to memorize for every word, that would probably be rarely said. So you probably just wouldn't know!
    </p>
    <p>
      This is basically how LLMs see the world. Obivously, they don't see symbols the way we do, but chunks of letters in the input are convered to "tokens", and then the output tokens are coverted back into letters. The most common tokenizer is called "byte pair" encodings, which is basically the algorithm we described above: start out with tokesn for each character, then combine the most common pairs of tokens into new tokens, and repeat until you reach your desired number of tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      The underlying reason is simple. Every LLM has a limited number of inputs. The naive approach is to use one letter per input. But it turns out it's hard to increase the number of inputs, and it's easier to increase the number of of letters there are. So, to get the most bang for your buck, you end up inventing thosands (or even millions) of new "letters", because each new letter allows you to fit more information into your limited number of input tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      Say yuu decide to have 32,000 tokens in your LLM. You can decide what each one corresponds to. So you suddenly have an optimization problem on your hands. To get the most bang for your buck, you want a series of tokens that minizes the length of most inputs to your modeel. Turning "the" into one token is very productive, because almost all inputs will contain "the". But turning an rarer sequence of letters like "aardvark" is less productive, because almost no inpnuts will contain that word so the presence of that tken won't be shortening them.
    </p>
